# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

Use this resource when each key needs its own Terraform address. Keys outside
those addresses remain unmanaged. To own the entire collection and delete
omitted keys, use `braze_sdk_authentication_keys`
instead. Do not use both resource types for the same app.

This resource registers public keys only. Keep private keys with the JWT issuer;
SDK integration and enforcement are configured separately using the
[Braze SDK Authentication guide](https://www.braze.com/docs/developer_guide/sdk_integration/authentication/).
The REST API key needs `sdk_authentication.create`, `sdk_authentication.keys`,
`sdk_authentication.primary`, and `sdk_authentication.delete` for the full lifecycle.

## Example Usage

The map keys are stable Terraform addresses, such as `current` and `next`.
Supply public PEM material in `sdk_authentication_keys` and set `primary_key` to
one of those map keys. Configure `primary = true` on at most one key per app and
use `null` or omission on the others. `false` is unsupported: Braze demotes a key
by promoting another key.

{{tffile .ExampleFile }}

## Rotation and deletion

Braze allows [at most three keys per app](https://www.braze.com/docs/api/endpoints/sdk_authentication/post_create_sdk_authentication_key/)
and [rejects deletion of the primary key](https://www.braze.com/docs/api/endpoints/sdk_authentication/delete_sdk_authentication_key/),
even when other keys exist.

For controlled rotation, keep stable addresses and apply each stage separately:

1. Add a replacement key under a new map key, retaining the old key.
2. Select the replacement in `primary_key` and apply. Refresh must report the old
   key as non-primary before it can be removed.
3. Migrate JWT issuers and allow the required overlap interval.
4. Remove the old map member and apply to delete the retired key.

Changing `app_id`, `rsa_public_key`, or `description` replaces that resource.
The provider rejects these changes for a currently primary key during planning.
Forced or tainted replacement may reach apply and fail when Braze rejects
primary-key deletion. `create_before_destroy` can collapse creation, promotion,
and deletion into one apply; it does not provide an issuer migration interval.
Use staged rotation when that interval is required.

Destroy deletes a non-primary key. A destroy containing a currently primary key
cannot finish through the Braze API. To keep the key while relinquishing
management, remove its resource configuration and use:

{{tffile "examples/sdk_authentication_key/relinquish/removed.tf" }}

The `removed` example relinquishes the entire `for_each` resource, retaining all
its keys. To relinquish a single instance, use `terraform state rm` with that
instance's full address.

## Import and recovery

Use the provider configuration for the Braze workspace that owns the app. Import
identifies both the app and key; it does not select a provider or workspace.
With the example's `current` map member configured, choose one import form:

{{codefile "shell" .ImportFile }}

Or use an identity import block with Terraform 1.12 or later:

{{tffile "examples/resources/braze_sdk_authentication_key/import.tf" }}

If creation returns a key ID but verification fails, the provider retains that
ID and reports an error. Terraform may mark the resource tainted. Inspect the
remote key and state after resolving the failure. Deliberately untaint the
verified key, or relinquish its state entry without destroying it and re-import
the same `<app_id>/<key_id>`. Replacement of a currently primary key can fail at
deletion and is not a reliable recovery path.

